    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0189" n="113" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg113"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5531/372</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Belgian Commercial Delegation</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Forthomme</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-21">February 21, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: Mr. Camu has just had an
                    interview with officers of the Department of Agriculture, and it seems from this
                    interview that if Belgium wishes to obtain the authorization to import bulbs
                    packed in sand into the United States, a formal request to this effect must be
                    sent to the Department in question.</p>
                <p>Since this is one of the points considered in the Belgian memorandum of November
                    12, 1934,<anchor n="21" xml:id="d86-f1"/> I have the honor of asking you kindly
                    to forward this request, in my name, to your honorable colleague the Secretary
                    of Agriculture.</p>
                <p>I have heard that this was one of the privileges recently granted Holland.<anchor
                        n="22" xml:id="d86-f2"/></p>
                <p>Please accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed>P. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Forthomme</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
